title: 'Fed, Full, and Forgetful: Three Warnings from Hosea 13'
author: richard-brashear
excerpt: We live in a culture that celebrates the “look how well I’m doing” season.
date: 2026-08-12
category: HOSEA
---
We live in a culture that celebrates the “look how well I’m doing” season. The promotion. The bigger salary. The family that looks just perfect online. If I’m honest, those are some of the seasons when I’ve drifted farthest from God.

Not the painful ones.

Not the ones where I worried about money or the kids.

The ones that felt successful and full.

Hosea 13 is not an easy chapter to read. It speaks about idolatry, guilt, death, and God’s judgment. The last verses are especially difficult because they describe the terrible consequences Israel would face because of its rebellion.&nbsp;

**Yet even in this dark chapter, we can see signs of hope.** We see a God who had rescued His people, provided for them, and remained their only hope in the face of sin and death.&nbsp;

Hosea 13 still hits close to home. What it describes as a danger was a recurring problem for ancient Israel and is still a present danger for us. Israel’s greatest problem was not that God had abandoned them. Their problem was that they had abandoned God. They replaced Him with idols, took His blessings for granted, and rejected the only One who could save them.

Although we may not bow down to statues made of silver, we face many of the same temptations. Hosea 13 gives us three warnings that still matter for our lives.

## **1. Remember the God Who Rescued You**

God reminded Israel, “I am the Lord your God from the land of Egypt” (v. 4). Israel had once experienced slavery in Egypt. They could not free themselves, so God stepped in and rescued them.

God led them through the wilderness, cared for them, and gave them everything they needed. However, something changed after they felt satisfied. God said, “They were filled, and their heart was lifted up; therefore they forgot me” (v. 6). Their blessings led to pride, and their pride caused them to forget the God who had blessed them.

We can do the same thing. When life is hard, we pray because we know we need God. We ask Him for help, direction, healing, or money to pay our bills. Yet, when circumstances improve, our reliance on Him might decrease.

When we stop and actually remember what God has done for us, it changes how we look at the good seasons. In Hosea 13, Israel forgot the Lord right after they were filled and satisfied. We can fall into the same trap. He’s forgiven our sins, carried us through hard times, listened to our prayers, and given us real hope through Jesus. Remembering keeps us humble. It reminds us that everything we have is a gift, and that we still need Him just as much when life feels full. That’s the same heart Matt Redman captures in “*10,000 Reasons*” when he sings, “Bless the Lord, O my soul,” and calls us to keep worshiping through every season.

## **2. Recognize the Idols That Replace Him**

Israel turned away from God and made idols out of silver (vv. 1–2). Hosea showed that this was a foolish action. Craftsmen created these objects and then people bowed down and worshiped what human hands had made.

These idols could not speak, hear, save, love, or provide. Because the people trusted in things that would not last, Hosea compared them to morning mist, early dew, chaff blown away by the wind, and smoke escaping through a window. Their lives had become as temporary and empty as the gods they worshiped.

Our idols may look different, but they can be just as dangerous. An idol is anything we trust, love, or depend on more than God. **Money** becomes an idol when we trust it for security. **Success** can become an idol when our personal value depends on what we accomplish. **Politics, relationships, comfort, technology,** and even our **own opinions** can begin to take God’s place.

Most of these things are not wrong on their own. The problem begins when we expect them to give us what only God can provide.

That emptiness made me go listen to one of my favorite Anastacia songs, “*Left Outside Alone.*” Although the song is about a human relationship, its title also describes what happens when we depend on something that cannot truly love or save us. Our idols may promise security, happiness, and belonging, but eventually they leave us outside and alone. God does the opposite. He invites us back into his fold, gives us a place with Him, and offers the kind of security our idols never could.&nbsp;

One practical question we can ask is, “What am I most afraid of losing?” Another is, “Where do I turn first when I feel worried or uncertain?”&nbsp;

Our honest answers may reveal something that has taken God’s place in our hearts.

## **3. Respond to the God Who Redeems You**

The strongest warning in Hosea 13 is that Israel rejected its only source of help. God told them, “He destroys you, O Israel, for you are against me, against your helper” (v. 9).

God was not the enemy Israel needed to escape. He was the helper they needed to return to.

Because Israel had committed many sins, **judgment was on its way**. Hosea describes Israel as an unwise son who refuses to come out of the womb at the right time (v. 13). The opportunity for new life was there, but Israel stubbornly refused to respond.&nbsp;

Still, God asked, “Shall I ransom them from the power of Sheol? Shall I redeem them from Death?” (v. 14).&nbsp;

In Hosea, these questions appear as Israel faces judgment. But Paul later uses Hosea’s words to celebrate the victory Jesus won through His resurrection: “O death, where is your victory? O death, where is your sting?” (1 Corinthians 15:55 ESV).&nbsp;

God’s judgment against sin is real. The last verses of Hosea 13 make that clear. We should not ignore those verses or soften their warning. Sin destroys, and persistent rebellion has consequences.

But judgment is not the only message. God sent Jesus to bear our guilt and defeat the power of sin and death. Jesus is the wise and faithful Son that Israel failed to be. He sacrificed Himself so that sinners like us could gain forgiveness and new life.

## **So What Does It Mean for Us?**

Hosea 13 is a warning for us today. It forces us to honestly look into our own hearts.

- Have God’s blessings made us grateful, or have they made us comfortable and forgetful?
- Have we allowed something **created** to take the place of our **Creator**?&nbsp;
- Are we actually responding to God, or are we still resisting the help He wants to give?

The Good News is that we do not have to rescue ourselves. Israel turned against the God who had always been its Helper (v. 9). But we do not have to repeat Israel’s mistake. Our Helper, Jesus, has come. He took the judgment our sins deserved and rose again, defeating death.&nbsp;

The warning is serious, but the invitation is still open:

**Remember** what God has done.&nbsp;

**Recognize** whatever is replacing Him.&nbsp;

And **respond** to the redemption He offers through Jesus, the only hope that never fades.
